{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-215952","model_call":16,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q2590"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q2590"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_57_1283188868#5_2360938783","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q2590","rank":6,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_57_1283188868#5_2360938783\" , \"end_char\": 4771.000000 , \"headings\": \"Off with their heads! 5 French Revolution executions that changed history Off with their heads! 5 French Revolution executions that changed history “Louis <cover>must die so that the nation may live.” Maximilien Robespierre King Louis XVI Marie Antoinette Jean Sylvain Bailly The Drownings at Nantes Maximilien Robespierre Follow us on Twitter and Facebook to get high on history \" , \"segment\": \"Robespierre once declared starkly that “terror is justice” but when he found himself on trial, </cover>he attempted suicide and shot himself in the head. The attempt failed and a hideously wounded Robespierre went to the guillotine on 28th July 1794. From monarchy to republic and beyond, the French Revolution was a time of enormous instability. As the guillotine blade fell and the Revolutionary Wars raged, a soldier began to make his mark on the battlefield, emerging to lead his country and become a military legend in his own right. That man’s name was Napoleon, but his is a story for another day. Glorious ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_01_1394957137#2_1920030353","rank":5,"relevance":3}]},"time":9},"model_calls":16,"document_judgments":12,"relevance_3":9,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
