{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-215952","model_call":21,"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ robespierre \"louis\" \"enemy\" \"republic\")"},"record":{"query":{"op":"query","query":"(^ robespierre \"louis\" \"enemy\" \"republic\")"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_18_4377168#9_5257778","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q2594","rank":1,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_18_4377168#9_5257778\" , \"end_char\": 9039.000000 , \"headings\": \"Maximilien Robespierre Maximilien <cover>Robespierre Contents Early life Early politics Jacobin Club Opposition to war with Austria The insurrectionary Commune of Paris The National Convention Execution of Louis XVI Destruction of the Girondists Reign of Terror The \"enemy within\" Abolition of slavery Cult of the Supreme Being Downfall Arrest Execution Legacy and memory Public memorials Street names Plaques and monuments Arras Paris and elsewhere Resistance units Screen portrayals Notes References Sources (selection) Further reading External links \" , \"segment\": \"His fellow pupils included Camille Desmoulins and Stanislas Fréron. In school, he learned to admire the idealised Roman Republic </cover>and the rhetoric of Cicero, Cato and Lucius Junius Brutus. In 1776 he was awarded first prize for rhetoric. He also studied the works of the Genevan philosophe Jean-Jacques Rousseau and was attracted to many ideas, written in his \" Contrat Social \". Robespierre became intrigued by the idea of a \"virtuous self\", a man who stands alone accompanied only by his conscience. His study of the classics prompted "},"time":104},"model_calls":21,"document_judgments":14,"relevance_3":9,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
